\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The regime-dependent pattern in \S\ref{sec:main-results} establishes \emph{where} SICA succeeds and fails. This section investigates \emph{why}: trace correlation quantifies the SC plateau (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), supporting diagnostics confirm the mechanism (Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}), and remediation analysis reveals a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Table~\ref{tab:independence} reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$. Qwen3-14B in thinking mode reaches $\kappa = 0.91$ ($K_{\text{eff}} = 1.09$). The four Mistral-7B FOLIO seeds produce $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ ($K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.8\text{--}2.2$), yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp---seed-level constraint quality, not independence, drives the variation. Two conditions are jointly necessary for positive $\Delta$: sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ (Qwen conditions with $K_{\text{eff}} \leq 1.30$ never improve) and sufficient SC accuracy (Mistral on ProofWriter with the highest $K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ but SC $= 39\%$ produces significant degradation). Even when both hold, sampling variance determines whether the constraint pool is discriminative.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Trace independence metrics ($K = 12$, sorted by $\kappa$ descending). $\kappa$: Fleiss' Kappa. $K_{\text{eff}}$: Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}). SC: Self-Consistency baseline. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). {*}\,$p < 0.05$; positive $\Delta$ values do not reach significance. multi-T $= T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$, 4 traces each.}
\label{tab:independence}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Condition & $\kappa$ & $K_{\text{eff}}$ & SC (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Qwen3 (think) & FOLIO & 0.91 & 1.09 & ${\sim}$84 & ${\sim}$0 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & LogiQA & 0.82 & 1.20 & 78.50 & $-$0.50 \\
Qwen3 & FOLIO & 0.77 & 1.26 & 84.80 & $-$0.49 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 0.74 & 1.30 & 75.49 & $-$0.98 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO & 0.66 & 1.46 & 75.98 & $+$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.3$) & FOLIO & 0.53 & 1.77 & 58.33 & $-$1.47 \\
Mistral-7B & LogiQA & 0.51 & 1.81 & 51.00 & $+$0.50 \\
Mistral-7B (s456) & FOLIO & 0.51 & 1.82 & 55.88 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B (s42) & FOLIO & 0.50 & 1.84 & 55.88 & $+$3.43 \\
Mistral-7B (s123) & FOLIO & 0.49 & 1.88 & 54.41 & $+$2.94 \\
Mistral-7B (s789) & FOLIO & 0.41 & 2.17 & 54.41 & $-$0.49 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.5$) & FOLIO & 0.52 & 1.80 & 58.33 & $-$0.98 \\
Mistral-7B (multi-T) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 57.84 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}1.0$) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 55.39 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & PW-D5 & 0.21 & 3.62 & 39.33 & $\mathbf{-2.83}${*} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Supporting Diagnostic Analyses}

Four additional analyses confirm the mechanism underlying SICA's failure. Extraction error taxonomy (Appendix~\ref{app:extraction}): 91.8\% of constraints are technically discriminative, but their discriminative direction mirrors the majority answer; 42.9\% of constraints are redundant across traces (Appendix~\ref{app:constraint-quality}), quantifying how co-sourcing from the same model produces near-duplicate formalizations. Removing the 8.2\% non-discriminative constraints yields zero improvement. Constraint ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:ablation}): on Mistral-7B ($n = 804$, FOLIO + ProofWriter pooled), MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 94.4\% of predictions, with disagreements split nearly evenly (18 vs.\ 17); the solver's weighted satisfiability computation adds no value over simple constraint counting because constraint weights carry information redundant with vote counts (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). Per-domain, MAX-SAT provides a marginal edge in the intermediate-SC region (59.3\% vs.\ count-only 56.9\%) but actively hurts in the Type~C regime (36.5\% vs.\ 37.2\%), mirroring the regime-dependent pattern of SICA itself. Hyperparameter sensitivity (Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}): $K = 12$ is the only setting with $\Delta > 2$\,pp among five $K$ values, and only $T = 0.7$ among four temperatures yields a positive effect; both peaks coincide with relatively low SC baselines rather than reflecting hyperparameter effects per se. Entropy decomposition (Appendix~\ref{app:entropy}): all SICA effects concentrate in high-entropy problems ($\Delta$ up to $+12.8$\,pp in the intermediate-SC region), but the direction depends on the accuracy regime---high entropy signals correctable uncertainty only when the model has partial knowledge.

\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

We test twelve remediation strategies: nine prompt-level spanning six dimensions (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}) and three method-level (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). All nine prompt-level strategies fail. Contrastive prompting reduces non-discriminative constraints from 8.2\% to 6.0\% but newly discriminative constraints still mirror the majority ($\Delta = -1.1$\,pp). Cross-model extraction fails in both directions: weaker models extracting from stronger traces yield 0\% discriminative constraints; stronger models extracting from weaker traces produce accuracy within $\pm$1\,pp of self-extraction. Only 1 of 4 temperature settings yields a positive effect ($p = 0.092$); multi-temperature sampling produces $\Delta = 0.00$\,pp.

\paragraph{Method-level interventions.} Three method-level strategies target different aspects of the independence bottleneck on Mistral-7B (Table~\ref{tab:method_interventions}). Independent Constraint Generation (ICG) bypasses trace-constraint coupling by generating constraints directly from the problem statement, but achieves only 74.6\% parse rate (vs.\ 97.1\% for trace-conditioned SICA; Appendix~\ref{app:constraint-quality}), reflecting limited standalone formalization capability at the 7B scale. ICG produces near-zero average effect ($\Delta = -1.96$\,pp and $+1.47$\,pp across two FOLIO seeds; $-0.50$\,pp on LogiQA; McNemar $p = .206$, n.s.). Paradoxically, among successfully parsed constraints, ICG produces objectively superior formulations: higher Z3 compilation (99.4\% vs.\ 96.5\%), lower redundancy (32.3\% vs.\ 42.9\%), and comparable discriminative rate (77.0\% vs.\ 81.4\%), yet yields no accuracy improvement across three seeds. Per-constraint-set MAX-SAT reranking yields $\kappa = 0.47$ (Eff-$K = 1.93$), nearly identical to standard SC's trace-level $\kappa = 0.44$ (Eff-$K = 2.02$): even when constraints are generated independently from premises rather than extracted from reasoning traces, the resulting answers exhibit the same inter-rater correlation as trace-based SC. This demonstrates that the independence bottleneck is not an artifact of trace co-sourcing but a property of the model itself---the same model asked the same question converges to the same reasoning patterns regardless of the generation pathway. The ICG paradox provides the most direct evidence that the bottleneck lies not in constraint quality per se but in the inescapability of same-model reasoning bias. Contrastive scoring generates pro and con constraints for each candidate but yields $\Delta = -9.31$\,pp on FOLIO ($p < 0.01$) and $-4.50$\,pp on LogiQA ($p = .160$, n.s.), with ${\sim}$21\% degenerate scores on both datasets; all 34 discordant questions produced non-degenerate scores (vs.\ 20.1\% degenerate in the full sample), indicating that scoring failures stem from systematic pro-bias rather than missing constraints; 62.7\% of Contrastive constraints are trivially satisfied by Z3 (Appendix~\ref{app:constraint-quality}), explaining the degeneration mechanism (Appendix~\ref{app:error-categorization}). Debate adds a proposer-critic-defense chain, producing $\Delta = -18.63$\,pp on FOLIO and $-23.00$\,pp on LogiQA, both statistically significant by McNemar's test ($p < 0.001$ on both datasets). Discordant-pair analysis reveals the asymmetry: on FOLIO, 60 questions flipped from correct to wrong versus only 22 from wrong to correct; on LogiQA, 64 versus 18. Debate failures occur not when the debate process breaks down but when it succeeds: discordant questions average 2.93 effective debate rounds vs.\ 2.52 for concordant ones, suggesting that more extensive same-model debate produces more confident but incorrect constraints. Three of six method-dataset conditions reach significance, confirming that degradation is systematic, not stochastic. Only 15 of 79 unique discordant questions overlap between Debate and Contrastive (Jaccard $= 0.19$), confirming that the two methods attack different vulnerability types while sharing a common root cause in the independence bottleneck (Appendix~\ref{app:error-categorization}). At the 7B scale, multi-role prompting adds cognitive overhead that degrades both reasoning and extraction quality.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Method-level interventions on Mistral-7B with McNemar's paired test. All produce neutral-to-catastrophic results. ICG shows two FOLIO seeds; $p$ for the first. {*}{*}{*}\,$p < 0.001$; {*}{*}\,$p < 0.01$.}
\label{tab:method_interventions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll rc rc@{}}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{FOLIO ($n = 204$)} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{LogiQA ($n = 200$)} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
Method & Target & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ \\
\midrule
SC baseline & --- & 0.00 & --- & 0.00 & --- \\
ICG & Trace coupling & $-$1.96\,/\,$+$1.47 & .206 & $-$0.50 & .706 \\
Contrastive & Scoring bias & $\mathbf{-9.31}$ & .007 & $-$4.50 & .160 \\
Debate & Role diversity & $\mathbf{-18.63}$ & ${<}.001$ & $\mathbf{-23.00}$ & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Independence-accuracy trade-off.} Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization} compares Fleiss' $\kappa$ across four aggregation methods on Mistral-7B FOLIO. SC ($\kappa = 0.44$), Diverse-Prompt ($\kappa = 0.40$), and ICG ($\kappa = 0.47$) cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, forming a same-model ceiling that neither prompt variation nor independent constraint generation can break. ICG is strictly dominated by SC: same correlation ($\kappa = 0.47$ vs.\ $0.44$) but lower accuracy (51.96\% vs.\ 56.86\%), confirming that bypassing traces does not bypass the model's reasoning bias. Debate alone breaks through to $\kappa = 0.15$ (Eff-$K = 4.44$), nearly tripling the effective sample size, but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp. This constitutes a Pareto frontier (Figure~\ref{fig:kappa-accuracy}): methods that increase trace independence destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy cannot break the correlation floor. The only demonstrated path past this frontier requires signal from outside the model's distribution, consistent with the oracle result (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) and published systems that bypass self-extraction \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, pan2023logiclm}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Independence-accuracy trade-off across aggregation methods (Mistral-7B, FOLIO). SC, Diverse-Prompt, and ICG cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$ (same-model ceiling); Debate breaks through but collapses accuracy. $\kappa$: Fleiss' kappa on raw answers.}
\label{tab:kappa_generalization}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccrr@{}}
\toprule
Aggregation & $\kappa$ & Eff-$K$ & $K$ & Acc.\ (\%) \\
\midrule
Debate & 0.15 & 4.44 & 12 & 38.24 \\
Diverse-Prompt & 0.40 & 2.21 & 12 & $\approx$\,55 \\
Self-Consistency & 0.44 & 2.02 & 10 & 56.86 \\
ICG & 0.47 & 1.93 & 10 & 51.96 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

The regime-dependent pattern in \S\ref{sec:main-results} establishes \emph{where} SICA succeeds and fails. This section investigates \emph{why}: trace correlation quantifies the SC plateau (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}), supporting diagnostics confirm the mechanism (Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}), and remediation analysis reveals a fundamental independence-accuracy trade-off (\S\ref{sec:remediation}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Table~\ref{tab:independence} reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$. Qwen3-14B in thinking mode reaches $\kappa = 0.91$ ($K_{\text{eff}} = 1.09$). The four Mistral-7B FOLIO seeds produce $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ ($K_{\text{eff}} \approx 1.8\text{--}2.2$), yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp---seed-level constraint quality, not independence, drives the variation. Two conditions are jointly necessary for positive $\Delta$: sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ (Qwen conditions with $K_{\text{eff}} \leq 1.30$ never improve) and sufficient SC accuracy (Mistral on ProofWriter with the highest $K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ but SC $= 39\%$ produces significant degradation). Even when both hold, sampling variance determines whether the constraint pool is discriminative.

